WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.502
-My next guest
is a very talented actor

00:00:02.502 --> 00:00:04.671
starring in the new movie,
"The Dry,"

00:00:04.671 --> 00:00:08.474
which opens in theaters
and on demand May 21st.

00:00:08.474 --> 00:00:11.010
Here he is, Eric Bana.

00:00:11.010 --> 00:00:12.078
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:12.078 --> 00:00:14.080
That's what
I'm talking about, bud.

00:00:14.080 --> 00:00:16.783
Hey!
-Hi, Jimmy.

00:00:16.783 --> 00:00:19.452
-It's great to see you, bud.
I like your room.

00:00:19.452 --> 00:00:21.020
This is the kind of
fun thing about Zooms.

00:00:21.020 --> 00:00:24.090
I get to see the room.
I like --

00:00:24.090 --> 00:00:26.625
-Well, I got to say,
it's pretty unique,

00:00:26.625 --> 00:00:28.194
just coming down
a flight of stairs

00:00:28.194 --> 00:00:30.430
instead of flying 24 hours
to be on the show,

00:00:30.430 --> 00:00:31.664
so this is pretty cool.
[ Laughter ]

00:00:31.664 --> 00:00:35.501
-You are -- What time
is it now in Australia?

00:00:35.501 --> 00:00:37.804
-It's about 7:30 in the morning.
-Wow.

00:00:37.804 --> 00:00:40.606
Thank you for doing our morning
show, "The Tonight Show."

00:00:40.606 --> 00:00:43.309
I appreciate that.
-It's a pleasure.

00:00:43.309 --> 00:00:48.481
-Is it 7:30 in the morning
tomorrow for me, or is it today?

00:00:48.481 --> 00:00:49.682
Who's living in the --
-No, it's tomorrow, mate.

00:00:49.682 --> 00:00:52.151
You're time-traveling.
It's tomorrow.

00:00:52.151 --> 00:00:53.552
What day is it here?
I've lost track.

00:00:53.552 --> 00:00:56.055
But, yeah, we're ahead, mate.
We're ahead on everything.

00:00:56.055 --> 00:00:57.490
We're ahead on everything.
Okay?

00:00:57.490 --> 00:00:59.592
So just -- you have to remember
we're ahead.

00:00:59.592 --> 00:01:00.659
-What's the future like?

00:01:00.659 --> 00:01:03.863
What do I have
to look forward to?

00:01:03.863 --> 00:01:06.766
-Well, I can tell you that
right now, even though,

00:01:06.766 --> 00:01:08.201
you know,
we're still in the pandemic,

00:01:08.201 --> 00:01:11.204
I'm able to go to the football
every week and watch my team

00:01:11.204 --> 00:01:14.006
play with 30,000
or 40,000 other people.

00:01:14.006 --> 00:01:16.842
So I guess that is what the
future looks like for you, also.

00:01:16.842 --> 00:01:19.579
-Wow, that's fantastic.
Can you also --

00:01:19.579 --> 00:01:23.216
Just six random lottery numbers,
just to give to us.

00:01:23.216 --> 00:01:24.317
[ Laughter ]
-Oh, no.

00:01:24.317 --> 00:01:26.285
-All right,
you don't want to do that.

00:01:26.285 --> 00:01:29.355
What have you --
How is quarantine treating you?

00:01:29.355 --> 00:01:34.761
Did you go a little crazy,
or were you good at home?

00:01:34.761 --> 00:01:36.295
-Do you know what?
I'd like to think

00:01:36.295 --> 00:01:39.932
that I represent a little bit
of a balance for your audience.

00:01:39.932 --> 00:01:42.569
And I'd like to make them
feel better about themselves,

00:01:42.569 --> 00:01:45.571
if I can share this, Jimmy.

00:01:45.571 --> 00:01:47.640
I did nothing.
[ Laughter ]

00:01:47.640 --> 00:01:50.443
I did not bake any sourdough.
[ Laughter ]

00:01:50.443 --> 00:01:53.880
I did not paint.
I did not start a podcast.

00:01:53.880 --> 00:01:57.816
I did not plant a veggie garden.
I did not upload a yoga video.

00:01:57.816 --> 00:02:01.787
I did not do a yoga video.
I did nothing.

00:02:01.787 --> 00:02:04.090
-That's exactly right.
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:02:04.090 --> 00:02:06.558
That's what I --
See?

00:02:06.558 --> 00:02:08.461
Wow.
And now --

00:02:08.461 --> 00:02:13.833
Now that you can do something,
what are you doing?

00:02:13.833 --> 00:02:15.434
-Well, besides going
to the football,

00:02:15.434 --> 00:02:17.103
I think the first thing
I did was,

00:02:17.103 --> 00:02:18.971
I was just dying
to get out on one of my bikes.

00:02:18.971 --> 00:02:21.307
So I went mountain biking
with a buddy of mine.

00:02:21.307 --> 00:02:24.176
And we had such a strict
lockdown here where you couldn't

00:02:24.176 --> 00:02:26.846
travel more than 2 or 3 miles
from your home,

00:02:26.846 --> 00:02:29.949
and we had a curfew,
so when it broke,

00:02:29.949 --> 00:02:32.418
and you could get out,
it was kind of like crazy.

00:02:32.418 --> 00:02:33.619
It was like a scene
out of "Mad Max."

00:02:33.619 --> 00:02:36.355
It was like, "I just need
to burn some gasoline.

00:02:36.355 --> 00:02:39.592
I just need to just burn --"
It was crazy out there.

00:02:39.592 --> 00:02:40.726
It was just like
"Mad Max 2," you know?

00:02:40.726 --> 00:02:41.827
-Just piles of bonfires.

00:02:41.827 --> 00:02:44.397
-Whipping across the desert,
yeah.

00:02:44.397 --> 00:02:48.134
-I want to talk about
your new movie, "The Dry."

00:02:48.134 --> 00:02:51.370
Congrats. It's got amazing
reviews on Rotten Tomatoes.

00:02:51.370 --> 00:02:53.138
I think it's like 95%.

00:02:53.138 --> 00:02:55.575
It's a hit already in Australia.
[ Applause ]

00:02:55.575 --> 00:02:57.643
It is based off
of the best-selling book.

00:02:57.643 --> 00:03:00.579
-Yeah, so I read the book
by Jane Harper and loved it.

00:03:00.579 --> 00:03:03.015
And we just desperately wanted
to turn this into, like,

00:03:03.015 --> 00:03:06.252
a big cinematic version
of the story, which we did.

00:03:06.252 --> 00:03:07.920
And it was a huge hit
here in Australia,

00:03:07.920 --> 00:03:10.790
and we hope that American
audiences enjoy it.

00:03:10.790 --> 00:03:13.225
I tell you want, you can't
fly to Australia right now,

00:03:13.225 --> 00:03:14.494
but at the end of this film,
you're gonna feel like

00:03:14.494 --> 00:03:16.596
you spent a couple hours
in Australia

00:03:16.596 --> 00:03:19.031
in a little country town.
That's a promise.

00:03:19.031 --> 00:03:21.733
-So is it almost
also like a travel movie,

00:03:21.733 --> 00:03:23.903
is what you're saying?

00:03:23.903 --> 00:03:27.706
-Well, our depiction of this
fictitious town of Kiewarra

00:03:27.706 --> 00:03:30.075
is so accurate, you are gonna
feel like you were here.

00:03:30.075 --> 00:03:32.545
It is a whodunnit,
and I play a detective

00:03:32.545 --> 00:03:37.049
who's returning home because of
the funeral of his best friend.

00:03:37.049 --> 00:03:38.650
And we get
the impression straightaway

00:03:38.650 --> 00:03:39.985
that he's not welcome.

00:03:39.985 --> 00:03:43.656
And we find out that he's linked
to a crime in the past.

00:03:43.656 --> 00:03:45.658
So there's kind of like
two crimes, one in the present,

00:03:45.658 --> 00:03:47.893
one in the past
that need to be solved.

00:03:47.893 --> 00:03:48.995
And the audience
will have some fun

00:03:48.995 --> 00:03:50.763
trying to work out
what happened.

00:03:50.763 --> 00:03:53.532
-Yeah, and who sent you the note
and all this stuff.

00:03:53.532 --> 00:03:56.102
It's --
It's just insane.

00:03:56.102 --> 00:03:58.637
I love stuff like this.
I'm going to freak out.

00:03:58.637 --> 00:04:03.008
And we got a trailer sent to us,
so it's like you get a card

00:04:03.008 --> 00:04:05.912
that says, like, "You've got
to come to the funeral.

00:04:05.912 --> 00:04:09.482
You lied, Luke lied."
That's it.

00:04:09.482 --> 00:04:12.018
-Yeah, and it's
got this sense of --

00:04:12.018 --> 00:04:14.053
-I'm in, I'm in, I'm in, I'm in!
If I get that, I go,

00:04:14.053 --> 00:04:16.556
"What? What, what?!"
Wow, I'm in.

00:04:16.556 --> 00:04:18.757
-Yeah, it has that sense of,
like, having been forced

00:04:18.757 --> 00:04:20.860
to go to a school reunion
you don't want to.

00:04:20.860 --> 00:04:22.661
'Cause I'm having to return
to the country town

00:04:22.661 --> 00:04:24.730
that I grew up in
like 20 years ago

00:04:24.730 --> 00:04:27.934
and try and uncover the truth
of what occurred.

00:04:27.934 --> 00:04:29.835
And Genevieve O'Reilly is
in the film with me,

00:04:29.835 --> 00:04:32.304
and she's incredible, as well,
in this amazing cast.

00:04:32.304 --> 00:04:34.306
But you will feel like
you've spent a couple of hours

00:04:34.306 --> 00:04:36.909
in Australia by the time
you finish with this film.

00:04:36.909 --> 00:04:39.011
-Well, congrats
on the great reviews, buddy.

00:04:39.011 --> 00:04:40.346
And I want
to show everyone a clip.

00:04:40.346 --> 00:04:42.414
Here's Eric Bana
in "The Dry."

00:04:42.414 --> 00:04:43.749
Take a look at this.

00:04:43.749 --> 00:04:45.651
-I was just closing up, dude.
Do you want something?

00:04:45.651 --> 00:04:48.187
-No, I'm okay.
I'll just, um...

00:04:48.187 --> 00:04:50.223
My dad used to work here
when I was a kid,

00:04:50.223 --> 00:04:52.525
so I was just reminiscing,
you know?

00:04:52.525 --> 00:04:56.161
-Falk.
-Yeah -- Aaron.

00:04:56.161 --> 00:04:57.697
-Dr. Leigh, Chris Leigh.

00:04:57.697 --> 00:04:59.331
-Right.

00:04:59.331 --> 00:05:01.667
-So you knew the Hadlers?

00:05:01.667 --> 00:05:03.436
-Yeah, you know everyone
in this job.

00:05:03.436 --> 00:05:05.104
-Yeah, sometimes too well,
I bet.

00:05:05.104 --> 00:05:08.741
-[ Chuckles ]

00:05:08.741 --> 00:05:11.243
-Hey, I was wondering,
with, um --

00:05:11.243 --> 00:05:16.849
with Luke,
any obvious signs of distress?

00:05:16.849 --> 00:05:20.520
With Karen?
Bruising or anything?

00:05:20.520 --> 00:05:22.888
-You know I can't tell you that.

00:05:22.888 --> 00:05:26.091
Welcome home.
-Thanks.

00:05:26.091 --> 00:05:27.526
-Yeah!
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:05:27.526 --> 00:05:29.128
Come on!

00:05:29.128 --> 00:05:32.231
Eric Bana, everybody!
Check out "The Dry,"

00:05:32.231 --> 00:05:34.667
in theaters and on demand
this Friday.

00:05:34.667 --> 00:05:36.736
Have the best day, bud!
Thanks you so much

00:05:36.736 --> 00:05:38.071
for coming on our show.
-Thank you, mate.

